WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Ivan Iron Man Stewart Super Off-Road.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.380
If you like fast action, this game is high gear competition.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:32.180
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.300
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.540
And I'm Joe.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.520
And yes, that was the back of the box, but it was also an alleged quote from Ivan Stewart

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:48.360
himself. The, if you like fast action, this game is high gear competition, which if he actually,

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.120
forget about if he played the game or not, if he said that, that's pretty cool.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.680
That would be really cool.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.780
Like I would, I would want that.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.860
You think he said that like outside of the context of this game, he was just talking about something

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.220
and they quoted him for the game?

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.240
Yeah. He was just talking about off-road racing and he, and then they were like, cool, this would

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.540
work for the game. Oh, okay.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.980
I thought maybe he was talking about like Chinese checkers or something or like Monopoly.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:15.880
No, it would be, I mean, I think it would be really weird to ask like your, your cover

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.080
athlete or whatever to like write the copy, but maybe he just really wanted to.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.860
Yeah. Maybe he's also like thinking about his career after off-road racing and thinks that a cushy

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.300
marketing job might be where it's at.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.760
I, that might've been what happened.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:34.880
Who knows? The back of the box also says that this is the first four player simultaneous action game,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:35.980
which is not true.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:41.260
So who knows if he even said that quote, because we can't even trust the back of the box to,

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:42.800
you know, tell kids the truth.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:48.880
So is this game actually about super off-road racing or are they lying about that too?

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.220
It is definitely about off-road racing, whether it's super or not, I guess is what the essential

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.160
games list is for later on.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:01.720
But, uh, just to clarify on the four player simultaneous action, the back of the box lies

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.000
to you and you buy the game based on that assumption of like, wow, the first time four

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.840
players, even though there's probably a few four player NES games at this point.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:16.200
But the manual then clarifies that it's the first time four people can race together on

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.260
an NES. Ah, I see.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:23.020
But you'd have to buy the game, open it up, read the manual.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.880
Something that I mean, like how many, how often if we play 10 games, how many times you're

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.440
reading the manual? I used to read it all, but.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.520
But like as a kid, if it was a game I was excited for reading that manual was on the way

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.480
home from blockbuster was great.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:48.640
Wait, so you're telling me, Mike, that if you, you bought this game and they claimed that it was the first time, but it wasn't, but they still did what they promised.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.180
You would be, you'd be upset.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.260
Like, oh, this isn't the first time.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.480
I thought you were going to say like, no, you technically can't play four players.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:56.660
And then I'd be upset.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.280
Yeah. It's all about just validity here.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:00.500
Okay. I'm holding them.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.720
I'm fact checking them.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:07.240
Uh, yes, it's true that it one, it doesn't matter because you're still getting four player action, which is great.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:14.060
And then number two, Joe, you mentioned that, uh, if it's a game you're excited for, you would have looked forward to reading the manual.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:20.780
I don't know if I would have read the manual for, even if I got this game for this kind of game, you know, like a racing game.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.940
I feel like this is just kind of something that like you just turn on your drive a couple of laps.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.260
You hope you get first place.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.140
You turn it off.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.600
You revisit it in a week.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:41.040
Yeah. And also like, we've had some cool manuals, but, but I feel like, you know, I grew up more in a time where manuals were like there for you to enjoy.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.260
Yeah. They're part of the packaging.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.760
Yeah. A lot of the, these manuals we've seen on the NES are literally like instructions.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:53.260
So maybe it wasn't as exciting for, for some of these things to read, like press the B button to do this on the car ride home.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.760
This game was published by Trade West.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:08.040
And the only reason I bring that up so early is because there's a certain commonality in Trade West games that I think that their marketing team has a certain belief as well about celebrity attribution.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.040
So we have played Ivan Stewart's.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:11.880
Are they Al Unser?

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:17.400
They are not Al Unser, but they are Magic Johnson's Fast Break, which we played earlier this month.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.380
And then they're also John Elway's quarterback.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:28.780
Oh. The funny thing about all these is that, you know, these games, specifically Ivan Stewart's Super Off-Road, I can't confirm about Magic Johnson's Fast Break.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:39.780
You know, these were arcade games that didn't have celebrity attribution, but to bring them home, they needed the extra oomph, you know, like, well, this game would never sell if it was just off-road racing.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.620
We need Ivan Iron Man Stewart behind the wheel.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.060
I'd buy anything with Ivan, with Ivan Iron Man Stewart's name on it.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.480
Are you guys more familiar with Ivan Stewart or Al Unser?

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.620
They both sound made up to me.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.160
Yeah. Well, who has the cooler name?

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.560
Ivan. I don't know.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.060
There's something about Unser.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:06.960
Like, it just sounds so, like, salt of the earth that I appreciate that one more.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.220
Yeah, it's just that he's got Iron Man in the middle of it.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:15.200
Now, if it was Al Iron Man Unser Jr., that's perfect.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:29.940
Now, Trade West was the publisher, but Rare is the developer, and Rare already made a isometric racing game on the NES, and it was called RC Pro-Am, and a few of us voted for it to be on the Essential Games list, but not a majority.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.640
So it does not live on the Essential Games list.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.880
But are you surprised to find that out?

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:38.200
Were you thinking about RC Pro-Am at all while you were playing this game?

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:39.280
Oh, 100%.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:49.100
Yeah. I mean, it makes sense that they would, I guess, they would hire the people that did the game that was like this to make a game like this.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:58.120
Because I still have fond memories of RC Pro-Am, and this very much feels like that with, you know, key differences.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:12.820
And without being too obvious about the title itself being called Super Off-Road, there is a difference between, you know, kart racers, you know, things on a track, whether it's NASCAR or F1 or Mario Kart, right?

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:19.300
Like, there's a certain kind of expected experience on a track versus the off-road sports games.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:26.400
I think about, you know, I never played any of these 8 or 16-bit ones, but the Dirt series of games.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:33.860
That's a very hard racing game if you come from the kind of Gran Turismo world of things.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:38.340
Cars drive incredibly different on dirt than they do on a track.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:49.400
And so I'm just wondering, like, does off-roading excite you more than, you know, maybe the more competitive NASCAR F1 style racing?

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:57.020
Or is it more of a trade-off where you're like, well, this isn't the kind of racing I get excited about or that the majority of the public is interested in.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.260
So it's kind of just a niche category.

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:08.280
I mean, me personally, who is not really into racing of any kind in, like, the real world, I think off-roading sounds cooler to me.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:14.000
So, like, NASCAR, again, I could be totally wrong here, but it feels like it's just more about purely about speed.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.700
And this off-roading feels like it's a little more about, like, navigation and terrain.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:35.260
Obviously terrain, but, like, in navigation, like, taking these hills, taking these potholes, these jumps, and, you know, like, it's maybe not necessarily as much about, like, just how fast can you go.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:45.560
Yeah, I'd say that, you know, the Gran Turismo series and, like, the simulation racing games, they're just a bit too...

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:52.680
I mean, I still like those games, but they are much more, like, sanitized, and there isn't even, like, a damage model half the time.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:58.920
And so it just sort of feels like you are literally, like, racing toy cars sometimes.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:18.020
But I appreciate the games that try and step out the bounds of just straight motorsport, whether it is more of, like, a rally or off-road situation or going more fantastical and have it be ridiculous street racing.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.140
Like, I like it when a racing game has more of a hook than, like, we have every car.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:24.240
Right, right.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:34.880
And I think, similarly, it's like, if ESPN put on an F1 race, like, I'm more inclined to watch that just because of the high competitive level.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.600
Whereas if they put on the off-road racing, there's just something feels lo-fi about it.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:42.900
There's not... The stakes aren't quite there for me, and I wouldn't tune in.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.560
But in video games, racing is racing.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:56.740
Like, for me, super off-road and RC Pro-Am and, you know, if it was racing with jet skis on water, it's like, they're all the same kind of thing.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:05.360
It's like, you're driving a vehicle from the start to the finish line, and, yeah, it's totally different how it controls or whatever, but that's not the selling point.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.400
The selling point is the race itself.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.780
Would you play a running video game?

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.520
Yeah, I mean, I said track and field was a great game.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:16.220
We were just hitting A and B on that one, and I thought it was fun.

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:18.680
Oh, okay. Yeah, I forgot about that.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:21.300
Yeah, as soon as you said that, that's what I thought.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.260
I was like, I know he's going to go track and field here.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:24.980
But I do...

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:29.200
I don't know. I do think that, like, yes, obviously, it's a racing...

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:30.260
Like, racing is racing.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:42.160
But there's a difference to, like, the difficulty of the racing being this, like, maneuverability and dealing with the, like, obstacles on the road,

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:48.780
as opposed to, like, you have a largely clear space, and the difficulty is, like, how can you go faster than the other guy?

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:51.480
Like, it does feel like a different type of sport.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:57.860
And anyone who knows anything about NASCAR or any sort of racing is probably, like, cringing at how little I seem to know.

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.020
But, like, that's just, like, my layman's view of it.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:06.880
Well, Joe, I think you're spot on, though, about, especially for this 8-bit interpretation of off-road racing,

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:12.060
there's only so much that they can make to have off-road racing translate onto the NES, right?

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:18.680
The dirt rally games are crazy simulation-style games that I genuinely can't get a hand on.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.720
Like, I can complete the tutorials, but throw me into those actual races, and I suck.

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:29.400
But on the NES game, like, you know, super off-road, I was having a good time, and it wasn't fair, it wasn't that challenging.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:36.480
But the whole challenge that they've created around the game is how hard it is to control the cars in the dirt,

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:44.980
in this isometric view where turning is everything and making sure that you don't ram into other cars to slow down and stuff

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:46.640
becomes the key to victory.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:53.960
I think that they approached off-road racing, specifically with 8-bit in mind, the best way they could have.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:55.620
Yeah, no, I agree.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:08.160
So I actually have some history with this franchise where when I was a Super NES player when I was, like, you know, seven,

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:18.220
I had Super Off-Road the Baja, which is a much more, like, typical nowadays racing game where it's, you know,

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:24.920
behind the car and you're just going straight, kinda, but there's, like, turns and it's sort of like faux 3D.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.220
And the terrain is still very much part of the game, but because in this game specifically,

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:39.900
they're trying to put an entire track onto one screen, the terrain is much more, it's much more exaggerated,

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:46.200
and they kinda made me feel while I was playing that this is the first racing game that I've played

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.560
in which it feels like I'm crashing the entire time.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:56.720
Like, I'm just always in the, I'm in the process of wrecking my vehicle the entire time I'm playing.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:01.280
So I think they did a really good job, you know, putting that feeling together.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:08.220
Yeah, I mean, I agree, and I think that the control scheme adds to that a lot, too,

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:15.220
because it is like RC Pro-Am in which turning right will turn your vehicle to its right,

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:20.800
no matter which way you're facing, which is the only way I think you can really do it from this kind of view,

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:24.000
but it does take a moment to get used to that.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:30.660
Like, and in RC Pro-Am, for better or for worse, in RC Pro-Am, there was a little more context to that,

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:33.020
because it's like, you're controlling an RC car.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.260
Yeah, you're always looking down at it.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:38.240
Yeah, it's how it feels to control, remote control a car.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:45.720
But like, this is like, yes, you're controlling this real car, but still from this third-person, like, over-world view.

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:51.220
And I'm not saying that, like, makes a big deal that, like, there's, like, this cognitive dissonance

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:54.460
that doesn't exist in RC Pro-Am because of that, like, little bit of plot,

00:12:54.560 --> 00:13:00.340
but it did, it did just, it gave context to, like, oh, this is why this is so difficult in RC Pro-Am,

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.920
this is why it feels weird, or this is like, this was more apparent to me that it feels weird

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:09.680
because of a technical limitation, or not even technical, like, just because of a...

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:10.800
Design choice, yeah.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:14.320
Yeah, limitation based on their design, yeah.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:24.960
And there are some major differences between RC Pro-Am and Super Off-Road,

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.860
but I think Sean mentioned the biggest one, the one that is kind of a love-it-or-hate-it feature,

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:38.040
and that is that in RC Pro-Am, you only see, you know, what you're currently driving on

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:40.300
and maybe, like, five feet ahead, you know?

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:44.700
They have arrows guiding you along so that you can anticipate the turns and everything,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:49.700
but if I recall correctly, that was one of my complaints, was that the speed at which you drive

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:54.180
and the view at which they're giving you, there isn't really a lot of ability to anticipate,

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:59.840
especially with the arrows, the diagonals weren't perfect, they didn't draw, like, right angles,

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.780
instead they had diagonal arrows, so you had to just kind of anticipate quickly.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:09.800
Basically, in Super Off-Road, the entire track is presented to you at all times,

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:11.420
and it's a really small view.

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:15.780
So, yeah, you were playing with remote control cars in RC Pro-Am,

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:20.780
but in Super Off-Road, it's like you're watching from, like, the nosebleeds in the stadium

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:25.740
of what could be, like, you know, after this, the monster truck rally goes on, you know?

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:33.680
Exactly. And I think, for me, this was actually a pro to see the entire stage, to see where everyone is.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:40.940
It's definitely great to know that you're, like, if you're in second and the guy in first is really far away,

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:45.480
now might be a great time for a boost to try to catch up, or to say, like,

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:49.420
well, second place gives me enough cash that I'll just be content with that, you know?

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:53.900
Whereas in RC Pro-Am, I feel like I didn't have all that information in front of me.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:15:00.520
Yeah, this is sort of like they take the mini-map in the corner of any typical racing game these days

00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:02.380
and just blow it up to be the entire game.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:07.140
Because, you know, it's really just giving you that information,

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:11.000
but now there's little mounds of dirt that you can jump off of now.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:14.940
But is it too small to make controlling the car satisfying?

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:25.360
Are you doing maybe your turns are too round or you're driving too wild because of how small your car is on these maps?

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:26.360
That's just a question.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.220
I'm not accusing the game of anything.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:35.260
I'd actually say that the size of the actual vehicles on the screen,

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:43.440
I was pretty surprised by the amount of different variations of angles

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:49.020
and just different states that the vehicle can be in,

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:51.980
which I think is half the battle here.

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:56.460
Like, that's sort of how you're giving the information of how you're driving.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.100
is, like, if you're moving at a certain angle,

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:05.060
but they only drew so many angles that the car can look like it's in,

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:09.560
then it's going to feel wrong and you're probably going to make a lot of mistakes

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:12.440
because you think you're going in a certain direction, but you're not.

00:16:12.580 --> 00:16:17.900
Whereas I think that there's actually a lot of information being given to you here.

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:19.600
I liked the feel of this game.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:25.060
I sort of did too, like, despite what I initially thought I would feel,

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:32.000
because it is all so small and so zoomed out that you do lose a lot of the, like,

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:34.420
illusion of speed you would get otherwise.

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:42.760
And I guess I was surprised at the times where driving in this game was actually very exciting

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:47.720
because it doesn't, on paper, look like it would be very exciting at all.

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:50.800
It feels like you're going very slow because you're so far out.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.400
And, like, yes, you've got these obstacles, but they're so small.

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:56.120
They don't feel like, oh, I got a huge jump off of this.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.020
It just, you just see, like, it's like you're seeing, yeah,

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:00.220
like, it's like you're seeing that mini-map.

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:04.680
It's like you're seeing, like, a diagram or a chart of how much air you just got.

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:07.760
You know what I mean? Like, it's a little, like, boring-ified.

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:12.540
But, I mean, I will also say that, like, I watched some YouTube videos after playing too

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:15.980
where things got even more exciting that it never happened for me

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:17.800
because I'm just not good enough at this game.

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:23.920
But, like, the excitement comes from the, like, built-in feeling of, like,

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:24.900
I need to catch up to that guy.

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:26.140
I need to watch out for this thing.

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:32.380
And, like, you see the danger even if it doesn't, like, appear as exciting on screen.

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:36.040
So, like, I guess I'm kind of giving a pro and a con at the same time.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:40.000
Like, it does not look as fast, but I feel like they found ways to, like,

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:43.180
make it still feel urgent and fast-paced.

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:48.780
